Links whose address holds brackets

The chat reads the Markdown links in an answer with the pattern \[(.+?)]\((.+?)\),
which ends a link's address at its first closing bracket. A link to a file in a
folder such as "SI data (20)",
[acq 01](https://er-c-data-mgmt.fz-juelich.de/browse/?path=/adhoc/x/SI+data+(20)/Spectrum+of+Live+EELS+Spectrum.dm4),
ended after "(20": the rest of the address showed as text, and the dataset was
missing from the answer's references (staging 2026-11-12, @weber: 7 of 14 links).
CommonMark allows brackets in a link's address in balanced pairs; read links that
way, the pairs nested up to three deep. As in CommonMark, an address with an
unbalanced bracket makes no link and stays text. Nothing else of CommonMark is added
(no escaped brackets, no <…> addresses, no link titles).

- datasetCitations.ts: LINK_ADDRESS, the address pattern, in MARKDOWN_LINK (the
  answer's citation numbers and references).
- MessageMarkdown.tsx: the same in the link and bold-link forms of tokenRegex (the
  answer as shown, also in the PDF export).
- conversationExport.ts: the same in LINK (the reference numbers in the Markdown
  export).
- utils.ts, trimTrailingPartialLink: while an answer streams in, a link counts as
  complete once the brackets in its address are closed, not at the first closing
  bracket, so the rest of its address does not flash as text.
- Tests: the staging link, nested brackets, a link followed by text in brackets,
  an unbalanced bracket.

Not changed: stripMarkdown (utils.ts), which is for the Data Player's tool
descriptions, not the chat.

Applies to matchmaker v0.14.0 (e7138c898926ebb79e45dd7d20cbf838dcf5dbe5) with
patches 0001 and 0002.

